abstract:
A probabilistic classifier built on Bayes' theorem plus one assumption that is almost always false — and which works anyway. We derive the classifier from first principles, show precisely why the naive independence assumption can be wrong without hurting the arg-max, work a spam example by hand, cover the Gaussian, Multinomial, Bernoulli and Complement variants, explain Laplace smoothing and the log-sum trick, and measure where it breaks.}
